<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Karbala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/680">680 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Karbala · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-fitna">Second Fitna</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Karbala was fought in 680 CE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> between the forces of the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-i">Yazid I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a>, grandson of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>. Husayn and most of his small band were killed. The event became central to Shia identity and a lasting source of opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala.jpg" alt="Battle of Karbala" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Persian Painter Abbas Al-Musavi. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Battle_of_Karbala_-_Abbas_Al-Musavi_-_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the assassination of the third caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uthman-ibn-affan">Uthman ibn Affan</a> in 656, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-abi-talib">Ali ibn Abi Talib</a> was proclaimed caliph, but Mu'awiya ibn Abi Sufyan, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, denounced his legitimacy. After Ali was assassinated in 661, his son Hasan handed power to Mu'awiya by treaty, on condition that Mu'awiya rule justly and let his successor be chosen by consultation. When Hasan died in 670, his brother Husayn became head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Banu Hashim</a> clan. Mu'awiya named his son Yazid successor before his death in 680, making the caliphate hereditary.</p><p>Husayn declined to pledge allegiance to Yazid and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. There the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> sent him many letters inviting him to overthrow Umayyad rule, and his cousin Muslim ibn Aqil reported favourably on their support. Husayn set out for the city on 9 September 680 with about fifty men and his family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On the way Husayn learned that Muslim ibn Aqil had been executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, and told his followers they could leave. Most who had joined him on the road did, and his companions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> stayed. A force of 1,000 sent by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> intercepted his caravan, and he camped at Karbala on 2 October, where a further 4,000 Umayyad troops arrived. The governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubayd-allah-ibn-ziyad">Ubayd Allah ibn Ziyad</a> refused safe passage unless Husayn submitted to his authority, which Husayn rejected.</p><p>On 10 October 680 Husayn addressed his opponents, reminding them that he was Muhammad's grandson. Al-Hurr ibn Yazid al-Tamimi went over to his side. Husayn was struck by an arrow and then surrounded and killed. Seventy or seventy-two died on his side, and the surviving women and children were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Kufan supporters felt guilty at having abandoned Husayn and formed the Tawwabin movement, with the slogan Revenge for Husayn, which was crushed in January 685. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mukhtar-al-thaqafi">Mukhtar al-Thaqafi</a> then ruled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> for eighteen months, killing several of those responsible for Husayn's death. Heinz Halm writes that there was no religious aspect to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shi'ism</a> before 680 and that the deaths at Karbala created it. Shia commemorate Husayn's martyrdom in the ten days of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muharram">Muharram</a>, ending on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a>, and the Imam Husayn Shrine at Karbala is a holy site of Shia Islam, the destination of the Arba'in pilgrimage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Karbala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During it Husayn ibn Ali, Ali's son and Muhammad's grandson, was killed with a small band of followers at <b>Karbala</b> in 680 by forces sent by Yazid's governor of Iraq, Ubayd Allah ibn Ziyad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The killing of Ali’s son Husayn and his companions at <b>Karbala</b> in 680 by the forces of the Umayyad caliph Yazid became the founding tragedy of Shia Islam, mourned every year at Ashura.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>About 12,000 Wahhabis from Najd, under Abdulaziz bin Muhammad Al Saud of the Emirate of Diriyah, attacked <b>Karbala</b> in Ottoman Iraq and killed over three thousand inhabitants.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-21">21 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Husayn ibn Ali, grandson of the Prophet Muhammad, who had refused to recognise the Umayyad caliph Yazid I, was killed with most of his small band by Yazid's forces at <b>Karbala</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-10">10 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The family controlled the farming districts and the routes to the Shia shrine cities of Najaf and <b>Karbala</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mazyadid-dynasty">Mazyadid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
